# example_data/

This directory is where the notebook reads image data from. On first run,
the notebook's `load_data()` call will download a subset of CIFAR-10 into
this directory and save it as `cifar10_subset.pt`. Subsequent runs reload
that file directly.

CIFAR-10 contains 60,000 32x32 color images across 10 classes at 6,000
images per class. The smoke subset uses 10,000 training images and 1,000
test images.

## Use your own data

Drop a `.pt` file into this directory with the following structure:

```python
torch.save({
    "x_train": ...,  # (N_train, 3, H, W) float tensor — NCHW format
    "y_train": ...,  # (N_train,) long tensor of labels in [0, n_classes)
    "x_test":  ...,  # (N_test, 3, H, W) float tensor
    "y_test":  ...,  # (N_test,) long tensor of labels
}, "example_data/my_dataset.pt")
```

- Images must be 4D tensors `(N, 3, H, W)` in NCHW format (BGR channels,
  height, width). The normalization in `data.py`'s default transforms uses
  CIFAR-10 mean/std. For a different dataset, update the ` Normalize(...)`
  parameters in `build_transforms()`.
- Labels must be 1D long tensors with values in `[0, n_classes)`.
- The model in `method/model.py` infers `n_classes` from the paper's spec,
  so your test set labels must be consistent with that count.

## Paper-scale data

The paper evaluates on ImageNet-1K (1.28M training images, 50,000 validation
images, 224x224 or higher resolution). ImageNet is too large for smoke runs;
the notebook uses CIFAR-10 as a proxy to verify the architecture works.
For paper-faithful evaluation, download ImageNet and create a `.pt` cache
matching the format above.
